## TL;DR

| What you need | How to do it |
|---------------|--------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 6.9.0** (the version you already have) | No extra plugin – the engine now shapes Arabic/Hebrew itself. |
| **A font that contains the Arabic/Hebrew glyphs** (e.g. *Noto Sans Arabic* / *Noto Sans Hebrew*) | Add the font to the style’s `text-font` list **and** expose it to the map via `font-faces` (or serve its glyph PBFs). |
| **Correct `text-field` values** (real Unicode characters, not transliterations) | Nothing special – just use the native Arabic/Hebrew strings. |
| **Optional: `text-direction: "auto"`** (or `"rtl"` if you want to force it) | Guarantees the label is laid out right-to-left even if the surrounding UI is LTR. |
| **(If you are on an older GL JS < 6.9.0)** | Load the RTL-text plugin **once**, before creating the map. |

Below is a step-by-step guide that covers the whole pipeline – from the style JSON to the JavaScript that creates the map – and a short troubleshooting checklist.

---

## 1. Verify you are really on GL JS ≥ 6.9.0

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

console.log('MapLibre version', maplibregl.version); // should be >= 6.9.0
```

If the version is older, upgrade (e.g. `npm i maplibre-gl@^6.9.0`).  
*Only versions < 6.9.0 need the deprecated `setRTLTextPlugin`.*

---

## 2. Choose a font that contains the needed scripts

| Script | Recommended open-source font (free) | Where to get the PBFs (if you want a glyph server) |
|--------|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| Arabic | **Noto Sans Arabic** (`Noto Sans Arabic Regular`, `Bold`, …) | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/font/Noto%20Sans%20Arabic/{range}.pbf` (demo) or copy from the **openmaptiles/fonts** repo |
| Hebrew | **Noto Sans Hebrew** | Same as above, just replace the folder name |

If you already have a glyph server (e.g. `https://mytiles.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`), make sure the folder names inside the server match the **exact** `text-font` strings you will use (case-sensitive, spaces encoded as `%20`).

### 2-a. Using a glyph server (the classic way)

```json
{
  "glyphs": "https://mytiles.example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place_arabic",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "filter": ["==", "$type", "Point"],
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name:ar"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Arabic Regular"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-direction": "auto"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place_hebrew",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "filter": ["==", "$type", "Point"],
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name:he"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Hebrew Regular"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-direction": "auto"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*All you need is the `glyphs` URL and the correct `text-font` name. The glyph PBFs will be fetched automatically.*

### 2-b. Using **local-fonts fallback** (no glyph server)

If you prefer to avoid a glyph server altogether (e.g. offline apps, CDN-free deployments), you can rely on the **local-fonts mode** that GL JS ≥ 5.11 introduced. In that mode the `glyphs` field can be omitted **or** left pointing at a dead URL; the engine will fall back to the browser’s text rendering for any font name it recognises.

```json
{
  // No "glyphs" field at all – or keep it but point to a non-existent URL.
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF", "U+0750-077F", "U+08A0-08FF", "U+FB50-FDFF", "U+FE70-FEFF", "U+1EE00-1EEFF"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF", "U+FB1D-FB4F"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place_arabic",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name:ar"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Arabic Regular"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-direction": "auto"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place_hebrew",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name:he"],
        "text-font": ["Noto Sans Hebrew Regular"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-direction": "auto"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**Why this works**

* Since GL JS 5.11 the engine treats each entry in `text-font` as a **CSS-like font-family list**.  
* If a glyph PBF cannot be loaded (or `glyphs` is missing), the engine falls back to **TinySDF** for Latin-type glyphs **and** to the **browser’s native text layout engine** for any font that the browser can load via `@font-face`.  
* The browser’s layout engine knows how to join Arabic letters, apply contextual forms, and handle right-to-left directionality.

> **Important:** The fallback only works **if the font file is reachable by the browser** (CORS headers, correct MIME type, etc.). For native SDKs you must still serve glyph PBFs – the fallback is GL JS-only.

---

## 3. Make the map aware of the `font-faces` at runtime (optional but handy)

If you want to change the font list after the map has been created (e.g. switch between a light-theme and a dark-theme font set), call:

```js
map.setFontFaces({
  "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
    }
  ],
  "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
      "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF"]
    }
  ]
});
```

`setFontFaces` merges with any `font-faces` already present in the style JSON.

---

## 4. Ensure the label layout direction is right-to-left

The style spec has a `text-direction` property (default: `"auto"`). `"auto"` detects the direction from the first strong character, which works for Arabic/Hebrew strings. If you want to **force** RTL (e.g. you have mixed-script strings where the first character is Latin but you still want the whole label RTL), set it explicitly:

```json
"text-direction": "rtl"
```

You can also set it per-layer or per-feature using an expression:

```json
"text-direction": ["case",
  ["==", ["get", "lang"], "ar"], "rtl",
  ["==", ["get", "lang"], "he"], "rtl",
  "auto"
]
```

---

## 5. (If you are stuck on an older GL JS version)

**Load the RTL-text plugin once, before creating the map.** The plugin is a small JavaScript file that adds Arabic shaping and right-to-left layout to the engine.

```js
import { setRTLTextPlugin } from 'maplibre-gl';

// Load the plugin from a CDN or your own copy.
setRTLTextPlugin(
  'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-rtl-text@0.3.0/dist/mapbox-gl-rtl-text.js',
  /* lazy */ false   // true would load it only when needed; false loads immediately.
);
```

After the plugin is registered, create the map as usual. The rest of the steps (font, `text-direction`, etc.) stay the same.

> **Note:** The plugin is **deprecated** as of GL JS 6.9.0 because the core engine now does the same work internally. Keep the plugin only for legacy maps.

---

## 6. Full minimal example (GL JS ≥ 6.9.0, local-fonts fallback)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Arabic & Hebrew RTL labels</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // No glyphs field – we rely on local fonts.
      "font-faces": {
        "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
          {
            "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
            "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF","U+0750-077F","U+08A0-08FF","U+FB50-FDFF","U+FE70-FEFF","U+1EE00-1EEFF"]
          }
        ],
        "Noto Sans Hebrew Regular": [
          {
            "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf",
            "unicode-range": ["U+0590-05FF","U+FB1D-FB4F"]
          }
        ]
      },
      sources: {
        osm: {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://example.com/tileset.json"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: "arabic_places",
          type: "symbol",
          source: "osm",
          "source-layer": "place",
          filter: ["==", "$type", "Point"],
          layout: {
            "text-field": ["get", "name:ar"],
            "text-font": ["Noto Sans Arabic Regular"],
            "text-size": 12,
            "text-direction": "auto"
          }
        },
        {
          id: "hebrew_places",
          type: "symbol",
          source: "osm",
          "source-layer": "place",
          filter: ["==", "$type", "Point"],
          layout: {
            "text-field": ["get", "name:he"],
            "text-font": ["Noto Sans Hebrew Regular"],
            "text-size": 12,
            "text-direction": "auto"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [35, 31],
    zoom: 5
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the `url` values with the real locations of your TTF/OTF files (they must be CORS-enabled).*

---

## 7. Troubleshooting Checklist

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Arabic/Hebrew labels appear **as separate letters** (no joining) | Using a font that lacks the script, or the font file never loads (CORS, 404) | Verify the `url` is reachable, check the network tab for 200 responses, and ensure the `unicode-range` covers the code points you use. |
| Labels are **missing** (transparent) | `glyphs` URL points to a server that returns 404 for the requested range **and** you are on a GL JS version **< 5.11** (no local fallback) | Upgrade GL JS or add a working glyph server. |
| Text is LTR when it should be RTL | `text-direction` left at default `"horizontal"` (old style spec) or you are using an older GL JS version without the built-in shaping | Set

Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows the three things you need to do:

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** that points at a DEM tile service.  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that uses that source.  
3. (Optional) tweak the hillshade paint properties to get the look you want.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from a raster-DEM source</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (any style you like – here we use a simple OSM raster)
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        },

        // 2️⃣  DEM source – must be type "raster-dem"
        "terrain": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          // Example: MapTiler DEM (you can replace with any XYZ/WMTS DEM service)
          tiles: [
            "https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY"
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          // The encoding tells MapLibre how the height is packed.
          // "mapbox" = Mapbox Terrain-RGB, "terrarium" = Terrarium format.
          encoding: "mapbox"
        }
      },

      // Base raster layer (so we can see the hillshade on top of something)
      layers: [
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        },

        // 3️⃣  Hillshade layer – the only layer type that knows how to read a raster-DEM
        {
          id: "hillshade",
          type: "hillshade",
          source: "terrain",
          // Optional: you can limit the hillshade to a certain zoom range
          minzoom: 0,
          maxzoom: 22,

          // 4️⃣  Paint properties – tweak to taste
          paint: {
            // Light direction (0-360°). 315° is a common “north-west” sun.
            "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,

            // How strong the shading looks (default 1). 1.5-2 is often nicer.
            "hillshade-exaggeration": 1.5,

            // Shadow colour (default dark gray)
            "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.5)",

            // Highlight colour (default light gray)
            "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.5)",

            // Optional: accent the slope (0-1)
            "hillshade-accent-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.2)"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Optional: add navigation controls
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | What you need to do | Why it matters |
|------|---------------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ raster-dem source** | `type: "raster-dem"` and a `tiles` array that returns DEM tiles (Mapbox Terrain-RGB, Terrarium, or any custom format). Also set `encoding` (`"mapbox"` or `"terrarium"`). | Only a `raster-dem` source can be consumed by a `hillshade` layer. The `encoding` tells MapLibre how to decode the height from the RGB values. |
| **2️⃣ hillshade layer** | `type: "hillshade"` and `source: "<your DEM source name>"`. | The `hillshade` layer type reads the DEM, computes slope/aspect on the GPU, and draws the shaded relief. |
| **3️⃣ paint properties** (optional) | `hillshade-illumination-direction`, `hillshade-exaggeration`, `hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`, `hillshade-accent-color`. | These let you control sun direction, contrast, and colour of the shading. Adjust them until the terrain looks right for your map’s style. |
| **4️⃣ base map** | Add any background raster or vector layer underneath the hillshade so you can see the relief. | Hillshade is transparent where the DEM has no data, so you need something to see underneath. |

### Quick checklist before you run the example

1. **DEM tiles are reachable** – test the URL in a browser. If you use MapTiler, replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid key (or use any other public DEM service).  
2. **CORS** – the DEM tiles must allow cross-origin requests (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` or your domain). Otherwise the hillshade will fail to load.  
3. **MapLibre version** – hillshade has been supported since the early MapLibre releases; the example uses v4.4.0 (current at time of writing). Any 4.x+ version works.  
4. **Zoom range** – DEM tiles are usually limited to a max zoom (e.g., 14). If you zoom beyond that, the hillshade will stop rendering (but the base map will continue).  

### Common variations

| Use-case | How to adapt the snippet |
|----------|--------------------------|
| **Use a vector-style base map** (e.g., MapLibre Streets) | Replace the `osm` raster source with a vector source and add a `background`/`fill`/`line` layer stack before the hillshade. |
| **Serve DEM from your own server** | Host the `{z}/{x}/{y}.png` tiles (or `.pbf` if you use a custom format) and point `tiles` to `https://mydomain.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png`. |
| **Use Terrarium-encoded DEM** | Change `encoding: "terrarium"` and point to a Terrarium tile service (e.g., `https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png`). |
| **Add a second hillshade with a different sun direction** | Duplicate the hillshade layer, give it a new `id`, and change `"hillshade-illumination-direction"` (e.g., 45°). You can also set `paint` opacity to blend them. |
| **Show the raw DEM as a grayscale raster** (debug) | Add another layer of type `"raster"` that uses the same `terrain` source, placed **below** the hillshade. Set `raster-opacity` to something low if you just want to see the heights. |

### TL;DR – Minimal code you can copy

```js
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  tiles: ['https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY'],
  tileSize: 256,
  encoding: 'mapbox'
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'terrain',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 1.5
  }
});
```

Add the source first (or via the style JSON) and then the layer. That’s all you need to get a hillshaded terrain on a MapLibre GL JS map. Happy mapping!
